WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:15.330
<v Ira Glass>Florida is crucial this election. Florida, Florida, Florida. One of the people who thinks about that a lot is Kevin Sheekey. He's the guy whose job it is to spend $100 million that billionaire Mike Bloomberg has devoted to winning Florida for Joe Biden.
<v Ira Glass>佛罗里达州在这次选举中至关重要。佛罗里达，佛罗里达，还是佛罗里达。凯文·希基（Kevin Sheekey）是对此深感忧虑的人之一。他的工作就是负责花掉亿万富翁迈克·布隆伯格（Mike Bloomberg）承诺的1亿美元，助力乔·拜登（Joe Biden）拿下佛罗里达州。

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:25.380
<v Ira Glass>Sheekey is not intimidated by that task at all. He's spent lots of Bloomberg's money in the past. He ran Bloomberg's three successful runs for New York mayor and his campaign for president this year.
<v Ira Glass>希基对这项任务一点也不感到胆怯。过去他曾挥霍过布隆伯格的大量资金。他曾管理过布隆伯格三次成功的纽约市长竞选，以及他今年参加的总统竞选。

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:34.310
<v Kevin Sheekey>And Mike likes to say he gave me an unlimited budget, and I exceeded it each and every time. I actually don't intend to do that in the state of Florida.
<v Kevin Sheekey>迈克喜欢说他给了我无限预算，而我每次都超支了。但我其实没打算在佛罗里达州这么做。

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:50.680
<v Ira Glass>So why Florida? Of all the battleground states, why $100 million there? Kevin explained that a big part of it has to do with what's going to happen in other states. He pointed to all the times, in tweets and in speeches, that the president has said that mail-in ballots cannot be trusted.
<v Ira Glass>那么为什么要选佛罗里达？在所有的摇摆州中，为什么要在那儿投入1亿美元？凯文解释说，很大一部分原因与在其他州将要发生的事情有关。他指出了总统在推特和演讲中多次声称邮寄选票不可信的事实。

00:00:50.680 --> 00:01:12.280
<v Kevin Sheekey>Donald Trump has sought to delegitimatize all mail-in ballots, full stop. And clearly, will have a plan to contest those ballots after election day, as they are counted, in important swing states such as Michigan and Pennsylvania, which Joe Biden is currently leading in.
<v Kevin Sheekey>唐纳德·特朗普试图全面否定所有邮寄选票。显然，他会有一个计划，在选举日之后计票时，对密歇根州和宾夕法尼亚州等乔·拜登目前领先的重要摇摆州的这些选票提出质疑。

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.780
<v Ira Glass>Michigan will take at least three days to count their ballots. Pennsylvania is not much different.
<v Ira Glass>密歇根州至少需要三天来统计选票。宾夕法尼亚州的情况也差不多。

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:28.630
<v Kevin Sheekey>Trump has said, listen, I'm going to take every lawyer and every judge I have. I'm going to question every single ballot and try to toss them out. And just throwing out ballots by random could really move those states from Biden to Trump.
<v Kevin Sheekey>特朗普说，听着，我会动用我所有的律师和法官。我要对每一张选票提出质疑，并试图废掉它们。随便废除选票真的可能让这些州从拜登的优势转变为特朗普的优势。

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:45.340
<v Ira Glass>That's because more Democrats are doing mail-in ballots than Republicans are this year. Bloomberg's people have actually run the numbers and calculated that there are states who were throwing out 7% of the ballots at random for not being filled out correctly, which would flip a Biden victory in that state to a Trump victory.
<v Ira Glass>这是因为今年使用邮寄选票的民主党人比共和党人多。布隆伯格的团队实际上计算过数据，发现有些州随机废除了7%因填写不正确而无效的选票，这足以将该州拜登的胜利扭转为特朗普的胜利。

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:50.480
<v Kevin Sheekey>And that's a very dangerous proposition. And that's what we're trying to avoid by getting into Florida.
<v Kevin Sheekey>这是一个非常危险的提议。而这正是我们试图通过进驻佛罗里达来避免的事情。

00:01:50.480 --> 00:02:15.050
<v Ira Glass>Florida is a state that Donald Trump pretty much has to win. If he loses Florida, it would be enormously difficult for him to gather enough electoral votes to have re-election. And-- this is key-- Florida is a state that counts mail-in ballots as they arrive. They're counting them right now, already, which means we very well could know the results in Florida on election night. And if Florida goes to Biden, it might settle the race that night.
<v Ira Glass>佛罗里达是唐纳德·特朗普几乎必须赢下的州。如果他输掉了佛罗里达，他想要获得足够的选举人票以实现连任将极其困难。而且——这一点很关键——佛罗里达州在收到邮寄选票时就开始计票了。他们现在就已经在统计了，这意味着我们在选举之夜很可能就知道佛罗里达的选举结果。如果佛罗里达归了拜登，那晚的选战可能就此尘埃落定。

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:25.820
<v Kevin Sheekey>And our ultimate goal is to win the state of Florida so that we, quite frankly, don't have to have that fight in a state like Pennsylvania.
<v Kevin Sheekey>我们最终的目标是赢得佛罗里达州，这样说实话，我们就不必在宾夕法尼亚州那样的地方陷入苦战。

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:31.410
<v Ira Glass>So how did you pick 100 million? What was that discussion like?
<v Ira Glass>那你怎么选定了1亿这个数字？当时的讨论是怎样的？

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:39.590
<v Kevin Sheekey>You know, listen, I think it was ultimately what we determined we would need. It was the number that we thought we would make a difference.
<v Kevin Sheekey>你知道，听着，我认为这最终是我们认定所需要的金额。这是我们认为能产生影响的数字。

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:56.720
<v Kevin Sheekey>I called a guy who I know who is probably the smartest strategist on the Democratic side. And I asked him, how much would you need to spend if you wanted to be able to have an impact on a race, statewide race, in a presidential in Florida? And how much would you need to really put your thumb on the scale?
<v Kevin Sheekey>我打电话给一位我认识的，大概是民主党阵营里最聪明的战略家。我问他，如果你想在佛罗里达州的总统竞选中，在全州范围的选战中产生影响，你需要花多少钱？你需要花多少钱才能真正起到决定性的推动作用？

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:26.790
<v Kevin Sheekey>And he said at the time, well, Florida is an extraordinarily expensive state. It has a lot of large, expensive media markets. And to have any sort of impact, you really need to spend $20 million. And if you really want to put your thumb on the scale, you're going to need to spend $70 million. And so, this being Bloomberg, we rounded up.
<v Kevin Sheekey>他当时说，嗯，佛罗里达是一个极其昂贵的州。它有很多大型且昂贵的媒体市场。想要产生任何影响，你至少得花2000万美元。如果你真的想发挥关键影响力，你需要花7000万美元。所以，鉴于这是布隆伯格，我们向上取整了。

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:43.860
<v Ira Glass>Most of that Bloomberg money is going to TV ads and digital ads, dropped into cities all across the state, like a vast air war conducted from 30,000 feet. Kevin Sheekey says Bloomberg's doing daily polling to see what effect this is having, which is actually kind of hard to figure out. All kinds of things push voter opinion around.
<v Ira Glass>布隆伯格大部分的钱都投向了电视广告和数字广告，像一场从3万英尺高空发动的空中战役，洒向全州各地的城市。凯文·希基表示，布隆伯格正在进行每日民调，以观察这产生了什么效果，但这其实很难判定。各种各样的事情都会影响选民的看法。

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:56.450
<v Ira Glass>But Kevin says the numbers in Florida have moved from a tie between Trump and Biden to a small Biden lead. It's still within the margin of error, but it's a lead, 2 to 4 points, that the Bloomberg people find encouraging.
<v Ira Glass>但凯文说，佛罗里达的民调数字已经从特朗普和拜登的平局，变成了拜登的微弱领先。虽然仍在误差范围内，但这2到4个百分点的领先优势让布隆伯格的团队感到振奋。

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:09.800
<v Ira Glass>So that's the view from 30,000 feet. Down on the ground, that all looks a little different because of something so basic. And that's the coronavirus and how the two campaigns have responded to the coronavirus.
<v Ira Glass>这就是从3万英尺高空看到的景象。但在地面上，由于一件非常基本的事情，一切看起来就不太一样了。那就是新冠病毒，以及两个竞选阵营对新冠病毒的反应方式。

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:19.450
<v Ira Glass>As you may know over the summer, the Democratic Party policy was, do not go door to door. Do not hold events in person. Do not hold rallies. Republicans did all that stuff.
<v Ira Glass>如你所知，整个夏天，民主党的政策是：不要挨家挨户敲门。不要举行线下活动。不要举行集会。而共和党人把这些事全做了。

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:40.430
<v Ira Glass>And in Florida, even before the virus, they were doing more and registering more voters than the Democrats. In sheer numbers, there are more registered Democrats in the state than Republicans. But since 2016, the Republicans have knocked that lead down. It used to be 7%. Now it's just 3 and 1/2%. A quarter of the voters in the state have no party affiliation.
<v Ira Glass>在佛罗里达州，甚至在病毒出现之前，他们的登记选民工作就比民主党做得多。按绝对数量计算，州内的注册民主党人比共和党人多。但自2016年以来，共和党人缩小了这一差距。曾经领先7%，现在只剩下3.5%。州内有四分之一的选民没有任何党派倾向。

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:56.330
<v Ira Glass>So the Republicans were going strong before the virus. And after the virus hit the state, they resumed normal operations. But the Democrats were not going out to campaign. It was like one party was out there in the streets, visible. And the other was just not.
<v Ira Glass>所以共和党人在疫情前就势头强劲。疫情席卷该州后，他们恢复了正常运作。但民主党人没有出去竞选。就像其中一个政党在街头上清晰可见，而另一个却完全不见踪影。

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:03.260
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Lina Misitzis, has been talking to Florida Democrats about all that. And Lina, how are they feeling?
<v Ira Glass>我们的制片人之一，莉娜·米西齐斯（Lina Misitzis），一直在和佛罗里达州的民主党人谈论这些。莉娜，他们的感觉如何？

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:06.060
<v Lina Misitzis>Not great. Or at least, not the ones that I talked to.
<v Lina Misitzis>不太好。至少我谈过的人感觉不太好。

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:06.980
<v Ira Glass>Uh-huh.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:23.270
<v Lina Misitzis>OK, so there's this one voter I found, Marji Hope. She's in a suburb called Weston, which is in one of the bluest counties in Florida. It's Broward County, which is just north of Miami. But she says that the Republican presence is so much more noticeable that her house is somehow surrounded by Trump signs.
<v Lina Misitzis>好吧，我发现了一位叫马吉·霍普（Marji Hope）的选民。她住在韦斯顿（Weston）的一个郊区，那是佛罗里达州最支持民主党的县之一。是布劳沃德县（Broward County），就在迈阿密北边。但她说，共和党的存在感明显得多，她家周围不知怎么全被特朗普的标语包围了。

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:23.660
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.360
<v Lina Misitzis>And there's almost no Biden signs in her neighborhood.
<v Lina Misitzis>而且她住处附近几乎没有拜登的标语。

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:26.800
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.020
<v Lina Misitzis>Yeah, and she sees Trump supporters everywhere.
<v Lina Misitzis>是的，她到处都能看到特朗普的支持者。

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:47.180
<v Marji Hope>The frustrating thing that is happening is when I get texts from my friends with pictures saying, oh my god, a carload of Trump supporters just got out in my neighborhood, and they're knocking on doors right now. But we have been not able to do that.
<v Marji Hope>让人沮丧的是，当我的朋友发短信给我，附上照片说，“天哪，一车特朗普支持者刚在我家附近下车，他们正在敲门”。但我们却无法做到这一点。

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:57.740
<v Lina Misitzis>So four years ago, Hillary Clinton's campaign had an office near Marji's house in Weston. And that's where people would go to pick up signs, to phone bank, to meet up with each other.
<v Lina Misitzis>四年前，希拉里·克林顿的竞选团队在马吉韦斯顿的家附近有一个办公室。人们会去那里领取标语、进行电话拉票、彼此会面。

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:24.380
<v Marji Hope>Biden doesn't even have offices set up yet here, OK? We haven't been able to-- we haven't gotten campaign material. I mean, how hard is it to just put stuff on somebody's door? We could get Weston done. There's, like, 65,000 people living here. We could get them done in a weekend. And that's where the frustration is, is the minute we would put a call out, I know that I'd get a ton of people to come out. Because we're all dying to do this.
<v Marji Hope>拜登在这里甚至还没有设立办公室，好吗？我们还没能拿到竞选材料。我是说，往别人门上放点东西有多难？我们完全可以搞定韦斯顿。这里有6.5万居民。我们周末就能搞定。这就是挫败感所在，只要我们发个号召，我知道会有一大群人出来。因为我们都迫不及待地想做这些。

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:35.900
<v Lina Misitzis>Not only is Marji keeping track of what her site isn't doing, she's keeping track of what the other side is doing. She actually signed up for her county-- the Broward County local Republican newsletter.
<v Lina Misitzis>马吉不仅在关注自己的一方没在做什么，她也在跟踪另一方在做什么。她甚至注册了她所在县——布劳沃德县当地共和党的通讯。

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:37.520
<v Ira Glass>Wait, why?
<v Ira Glass>等等，为什么？

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:52.010
<v Lina Misitzis>Because every day, when it sends out its daily email that lists all of the campaign events that they have coming up for the Republicans, she likes to keep track of it. And it kind of drives her crazy, just noticing all of these events that they're running.
<v Lina Misitzis>因为每天当他们发送列出共和党即将举行的所有竞选活动的每日电子邮件时，她喜欢跟踪这些信息。而这让她有点抓狂，因为她注意到了他们正在开展的所有这些活动。

00:06:52.010 --> 00:07:16.290
<v Marji Hope>They are doing candidate forums. They are doing watch parties for the debates. They had watch parties at this place my husband and I used to go have breakfast at all the time, which we're not going to anymore. Because I'm like, if they're hosting these huge watch-- I could send you all these pictures of these people at the debates. Probably fit a lot of young people--
<v Marji Hope>他们正在举行候选人论坛。他们正在举行辩论观看派对。他们在我丈夫和我以前经常去吃早餐的地方举办观看派对，我们现在再也不去了。因为我想，如果他们在举办这种大型观看活动——我可以发给你所有这些他们在辩论现场的照片。可能塞进了许多年轻人——

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.730
<v Julie>They make a lot of noise, and they make their presence known.
<v Julie>他们制造了很大的动静，让人们知道他们的存在。

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:29.190
<v Lina Misitzis>I also talked to Marji's friend, Julie. She tracks the Republican events, too. And while Marji sounds kind of indignant when she talks about it, Julie sounds almost wistful.
<v Lina Misitzis>我还和马吉的朋友朱莉聊了聊。她也追踪共和党的活动。虽然马吉谈到这些时听起来有些愤慨，但朱莉听起来近乎渴望。

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:44.970
<v Julie>They had pictures on the community Facebook page, and they had a watch party. And I thought, well, that's nice. I mean, most of them weren't wearing masks. So I know that that's something that a lot of people would be hesitant to do.
<v Julie>他们在社区Facebook页面上有照片，他们举行了观看派对。我想，嗯，那很好。我是说，他们中大多数人没戴口罩。所以我知道那是很多人会犹豫去做的事情。

00:07:44.970 --> 00:08:00.860
<v Julie>But it just seemed like it was something that involved community, that they were sitting together around, going, hey, we are a group. We are here. And they kind of support each other. And it's kind of something that I wish we could replicate and were as strong.
<v Julie>但这看起来就像是一种涉及社区的事情，他们坐在一起，说，嘿，我们是一个团体。我们在这里。他们互相支持。这就是我希望我们也能复制并做到一样强大的地方。

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:10.850
<v Lina Misitzis>In the absence of their own official meetups, I found a bunch of frustrated Democrats all over Florida, not just in Broward, who took matters into their own hands.
<v Lina Misitzis>在没有官方聚会的情况下，我在佛罗里达各地发现了许多沮丧的民主党人，不仅仅是在布劳沃德，他们自己采取了行动。

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.000
<v Ira Glass>What are they doing?
<v Ira Glass>他们都在做什么？

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:22.580
<v Lina Misitzis>OK, there's a woman in Oviedo, which is a small red town. And she's been hosting these very small rallies on street corners every Monday, Wednesday, and Friday.
<v Lina Misitzis>好吧，奥维耶多（Oviedo）有一个女人，那是个红色小镇。她每周一、周三和周五都会在街角举行非常小型的集会。

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:24.060
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow.
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:36.260
<v Lina Misitzis>Another example is a grandmother in Tallahassee who told me that she printed out a bunch of Florida voter registration forms. And she was handing them out to young-seeming people in the Walmart parking lot.
<v Lina Misitzis>另一个例子是塔拉哈西（Tallahassee）的一位祖母，她告诉我她打印了一堆佛罗里达选民登记表。她在沃尔玛的停车场把它们发给看起来很年轻的人。

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:43.490
<v Ira Glass>Now I've read that the Biden campaign did finally start going door to door in Florida and in other states, too, this fall.
<v Ira Glass>但我读到过拜登竞选团队今年秋天终于开始在佛罗里达和其他州挨家挨户敲门了。

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:50.540
<v Lina Misitzis>Yes, that happened two weeks after I started talking to Marji. And she went out with them. But for her, it wasn't enough.
<v Lina Misitzis>是的，那是在我开始和马吉谈话两周后发生的。她和他们一起出去了。但对她来说，这还不够。

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:57.540
<v Marji Hope>Why am I canvassing 23 days before-- whatever-- 25 days before an election? I should have been canvassing 40 days before an election.
<v Marji Hope>我为什么要在大选前23天——不管怎样——大选前25天拉票？我应该在大选前40天就开始拉票的。

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:58.930
<v Lina Misitzis>Right.
<v Lina Misitzis>对。

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:09.980
<v Marji Hope>You know how many people-- I mean, hundreds of thousands of people have already voted in Florida.
<v Marji Hope>你知道有多少人——我是说，佛罗里达已经有几十万人投过票了。

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:30.520
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our show, we have stories of people fretting, worried about what is going to happen next, and all that is still unknown. The future is barreling towards them. They're going to find out soon enough, but not quite yet. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And let's get to it. Act 1, the School of Hard Knocks.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的节目中，我们要讲述人们焦虑的故事，担心接下来会发生什么，而一切仍然未知。未来正在疾驰而来。他们很快就会知道结果，但还不是现在。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。让我们开始吧。第一幕，社会大学。

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:43.940
<v Ira Glass>So Lina looked into whether the fears that she heard from Florida's Democrats are justified. Are their leaders dropping the ball? Did the party pull back too far with the coronavirus precautions? She put together this story.
<v Ira Glass>所以莉娜调查了她从佛罗里达民主党人那里听到的担忧是否合理。他们的领导人是否在失职？该政党是否在新冠疫情的预防措施上做得太过了？她制作了这个故事。

00:09:43.940 --> 00:10:01.540
<v Lina Misitzis>I think Democrats have been doing, instead of door knocking, phone calling. And gone is the camaraderie of organizing, everyone breathing in the same air in some makeshift, fluorescent-lit campaign center. This volunteering is done from home. And here's what it looks like.
<v Lina Misitzis>我想民主党人一直在做的不是敲门，而是打电话。组织工作的友情消失了，每个人都在临时搭建、荧光灯闪烁的竞选中心呼吸着同样的空气。这种志愿工作是在家里完成的。这就是它的样子。

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:08.980
<v Lina Misitzis>Maria is a phone banking volunteer. She's 33, living in Southeast Florida with her husband and her three-year-old son.
<v Lina Misitzis>玛丽亚是一名电话拉票志愿者。她33岁，和丈夫及三岁大的儿子住在佛罗里达东南部。

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:13.900
<v Operator>Your entry does not match our records. Please enter your PIN, then pound.
<v Operator>您的输入与我们的记录不符。请输入您的密码，然后按井号键。

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:19.880
<v Lina Misitzis>She works in consulting. And after work, she has 45 minutes to phone bank before her kid gets home.
<v Lina Misitzis>她在做咨询工作。下班后，她有45分钟时间在孩子回家前进行电话拉票。

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:20.800
<v Operator>You are now connected.
<v Operator>您现在已接通。

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:28.900
<v Lina Misitzis>Maria was born in Colombia, moved here when she was 16 with her parents. She's a citizen now, and not a fan of the president.
<v Lina Misitzis>玛丽亚出生在哥伦比亚，16岁时随父母搬到这里。她现在是公民，不喜欢总统。

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:44.830
<v Maria>His comments against immigrants, against women-- I mean, I think almost every single position that he's taken and every comment that he's made is against what I believe and what I think this country stands for.
<v Maria>他对移民、对女性的评论——我是说，我认为他采取的几乎每一个立场和他做出的每一个评论，都违背了我所相信的以及我认为这个国家所代表的东西。

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:57.860
<v Lina Misitzis>Maria did door knocking in 2016, and she's doing phone banking now. I wanted her to compare them for me. And so I listened in while she phone banked. In 45 minutes, she dialed 28 phone numbers.
<v Lina Misitzis>玛丽亚在2016年做过敲门工作，她现在在做电话拉票。我想让她为我比较一下它们。于是我旁听了她电话拉票的过程。在45分钟里，她拨打了28个电话号码。

00:10:57.860 --> 00:10:59.420
<v Maria>Hi, is this Angie?
<v Maria>嗨，是安吉吗？

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:02.560
<v Operator>At the tone, please record your message. When you finish recording, you may--
<v Operator>在听到提示音后，请录下您的留言。当您录音完成时，您可以——

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:13.450
<v Lina Misitzis>Nine of those 28 phone calls went to voicemail, which the Democrats mostly don't think it's worth it to leave messages after the beep. One phone call, a toddler picked up.
<v Lina Misitzis>在这28个电话中，有9个转入了语音信箱，而民主党人大多认为在哔声后留言不值得。有一个电话是一个蹒跚学步的孩子接的。

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:14.770
<v Maria>Hi, is this Caitlin?
<v Maria>嗨，是凯特琳吗？

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:16.450
<v Toddler>Yeah.
<v Toddler>是。

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:19.460
<v Lina Misitzis>The kiddo seemed to put the phone down and walk away.
<v Lina Misitzis>孩子似乎放下电话走开了。

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.190
<v Maria>Hello?
<v Maria>喂？

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:24.110
<v Lina Misitzis>Some grownups didn't want to get into it either.
<v Lina Misitzis>有些成年人也不想谈论这个。

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:25.060
<v Woman>With who?
<v Woman>跟谁？

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:28.610
<v Maria>With the Democratic Party of Florida.
<v Maria>跟佛罗里达州民主党。

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:30.550
<v Woman>OK, thank you. Not interested.
<v Woman>好的，谢谢。没兴趣。

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:32.970
<v Maria>All right, thank you.
<v Maria>好的，谢谢。

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:36.610
<v Lina Misitzis>Of course, not everyone Maria spoke to favored her side.
<v Lina Misitzis>当然，玛丽亚交谈的对象并非都支持她这一方。

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.910
<v Man 1>I'm going to vote for Trump.
<v Man 1>我要投给特朗普。

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.160
<v Maria>Oh, you will?
<v Maria>哦，你会吗？

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:41.790
<v Man 1>I'm voting for Trump!
<v Man 1>我要投给特朗普！

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:42.240
<v Maria>OK.
<v Maria>好的。

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:44.210
<v Man 1>I can tell you that right now.
<v Man 1>我现在就可以告诉你。

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.200
<v Maria>Bye.
<v Maria>再见。

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.080
<v Lina Misitzis>There were three calls like that.
<v Lina Misitzis>有三个这样的电话。

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:56.100
<v Man 2>I don't think I want to vote for Sleepy Joe. I'm sorry. I don't think he's worth being president.
<v Man 2>我想我不想投给“瞌睡乔”。抱歉。我不认为他配当总统。

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:57.960
<v Maria>Thank you.
<v Maria>谢谢。

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:13.570
<v Lina Misitzis>The goal of these calls, it's not to persuade anyone. It's just to get Biden voters to vote. And over a third of them, 11 people, were Biden supporters. Six of them said that they'd already mailed in their ballots. And five of them hadn't.
<v Lina Misitzis>这些电话的目的不是说服任何人。只是为了让拜登的支持者投票。而在他们之中，有超过三分之一，即11人是拜登的支持者。其中6人说他们已经邮寄了选票。还有5人没有。

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:24.500
<v Man 3>Well, it's only the first week of October. They don't vote until November 3rd. I thought maybe I could send it out at the end of the month.
<v Man 3>嗯，这只是十月的第一个星期。他们直到11月3日才投票。我想也许我可以等到月底再寄出。

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:42.900
<v Maria>No, don't wait till the end of the month, because a lot of people are going to be mailing their ballots. And the Postal Service is going to be overwhelmed with the ballots. So make sure you send it in sooner. You don't want to have it delayed and not being counted.
<v Maria>不，不要等到月底，因为很多人都会寄送选票。邮政服务会被选票淹没。所以一定要尽快寄出。你不想让它延迟而导致无法计票。

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.160
<v Man 3>OK.
<v Man 3>好的。

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:50.940
<v Maria>Use black ink, sign the outside of the envelope, and mail it back quickly.
<v Maria>用黑色墨水笔，在信封外面签名，然后尽快寄回。

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.620
<v Man 3>OK.
<v Man 3>好的。

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:56.550
<v Lina Misitzis>Maria says that that one call, it made it feel worth her time that day.
<v Lina Misitzis>玛丽亚说那个电话让她觉得当天的时间花得值。

00:12:56.550 --> 00:13:04.200
<v Maria>Because I think that was one more vote-- one vote that will be counted and wouldn't have been otherwise if I hadn't called him.
<v Maria>因为我认为那是又多了一票——如果我没给他打电话，这一票就不会被统计进去。

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.780
<v Maria>All right, have a good night.
<v Maria>好的，祝你有美好的一晚。

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:08.680
<v Man 3>Bye.
<v Man 3>再见。

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:10.450
<v Maria>Bye.
<v Maria>再见。

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:21.040
<v Lina Misitzis>So 45 minutes, one actual vote for her team, for her state. Though, also, four other Biden voters that she nudged towards mailing in their ballots.
<v Lina Misitzis>所以45分钟，为她的团队，为她的州争取到了一张实际的选票。尽管还有其他四位拜登选民被她推动去邮寄选票。

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:36.040
<v Lina Misitzis>And how do these phone calls compare to door knocking? Maria says in 2016, in terms of how many people she'd made direct contact with in a shift, door knocking and phone banking are about the same. But going door to door takes way more time.
<v Lina Misitzis>那么这些电话与敲门相比如何？玛丽亚说，在2016年，就她在一次班次中直接接触的人数而言，敲门和电话拉票差不多。但挨家挨户敲门花费的时间要多得多。

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:48.340
<v Maria>Between going and grabbing the list and getting to the place and walking around and waiting-- someone's mom would open the door, and then we had to wait for the person that we were actually trying to reach to come over.
<v Maria>在获取名单、到达目的地、走动、等待的过程中——别人的妈妈开了门，然后我们不得不等我们真正试图联系的人走过来。

00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:56.000
<v Lina Misitzis>What happened yesterday took 45 minutes, and then what you're-- like, a Saturday afternoon in 2016, it sounds like it would take a few hours.
<v Lina Misitzis>昨天发生的事情只花了45分钟，而2016年一个周六下午的情况听起来需要几个小时。

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.810
<v Maria>Yeah, but I do think it's just as good.
<v Maria>是的，但我确实认为它同样有效。

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:12.650
<v Lina Misitzis>There's been actual research on this, which shows that door knocking and phone banking are pretty close in terms of their effectiveness, with a slight edge towards door knocking, though calling people is faster.
<v Lina Misitzis>对此有实际的研究，表明敲门和电话拉票在有效性方面相当接近，敲门略占优势，尽管打电话更快。

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:31.000
<v Lina Misitzis>So the question really is, how many phone calls are Democrats making for Biden in Florida, compared to doors knocked on in the past? I called around for numbers. The closest I got was from the independent organization that coordinates a lot of the get-out-the-vote among Democrats in Florida. They're called America Votes.
<v Lina Misitzis>所以问题实际上是，与过去敲门的数量相比，民主党人为拜登在佛罗里达打了多少电话？我到处询问数据。我得到的最接近的数据来自一个协调佛罗里达民主党人拉票工作的独立组织。他们叫“美国投票”（America Votes）。

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:46.030
<v Lina Misitzis>They say when you count phone banking and door knocking and everything else that they do to reach voters, they've doubled the number of people they've had conversations with in Florida. It's 2 million this year versus 1 million or fewer in previous elections.
<v Lina Misitzis>他们说，当你把电话拉票、敲门以及他们为联系选民所做的一切加在一起时，他们在佛罗里达进行谈话的人数翻了一番。今年是200万人，而在之前的选举中是100万或更少。

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:57.220
<v Lina Misitzis>But that number doesn't include the Democratic Party's own get-out-the-vote effort, which is an important part of the picture. Party officials would not give me outreach numbers for this year or previous elections.
<v Lina Misitzis>但这个数字不包括民主党自己获取选票的努力，这是图景的重要组成部分。党内官员不愿给我今年的联系人数或之前的选举人数。

00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:12.130
<v Lina Misitzis>What they did do, instead, is say that everything is on track. All of the Florida Democratic officials I talked to agreed on that. Here's Debbie Wasserman Schultz. She's Marji's congresswoman, and she used to chair the National Democratic Party.
<v Lina Misitzis>他们所做的是说一切都在正轨上。我谈过的所有佛罗里达民主党官员都同意这一点。这是黛比·沃瑟曼·舒尔茨（Debbie Wasserman Schultz）。她是马吉的国会女议员，曾担任全国民主党主席。

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:39.860
<v Debbie Wasserman Schultz>I mean, at the moment, I can tell you that we lead. I mean, the proof is in the voting. Democrats lead Republicans in vote by mail by 360,000-plus vote by mail ballots being turned in. That is just massive, massive increases. We are really lapping the field. And so what I say to nervous Democrats is, the proof is in the voting. And we have to just keep people voting.
<v Debbie Wasserman Schultz>我是说，目前，我可以告诉你我们处于领先地位。我是说，证据就在投票中。在邮寄选票方面，民主党以36万多张选票领先于共和党。那是非常巨大的增幅。我们确实遥遥领先。所以我对紧张的民主党人说的是，证据在投票中。我们只需要让人们继续投票。

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:53.540
<v Lina Misitzis>It's true that more Democrats than Republicans have mailed in their ballots. But that's kind of what you'd expect. COVID reactions have fallen along party lines, and way more Democrats asked for mail-in ballots than Republicans did.
<v Lina Misitzis>确实，邮寄选票的民主党人比共和党人多。但这正是你所预料到的。对新冠疫情的反应是按照党派划分的，申请邮寄选票的民主党人远多于共和党人。

00:15:53.540 --> 00:16:07.790
<v Lina Misitzis>Of course, things could get completely reshuffled on Monday. That's when early in-person voting starts in Florida. Republicans could start showing up in droves.
<v Lina Misitzis>当然，周一可能会彻底洗牌。那是佛罗里达州开始面对面提前投票的时间。共和党人可能会开始成群结队地出现。

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:30.830
<v Lina Misitzis>I honestly think that there's nothing Debbie Wasserman Schultz or other party leaders could say that would reassure Marji or Julie or any of the nervous Democrats I talked to. Jeff Green, who also lives in Broward, he remembers, two years ago, how confidently they all headed into the governor's race. It was the race that was supposed to show that a Democrat could win statewide office in Florida.
<v Lina Misitzis>我诚实地认为，黛比·沃瑟曼·舒尔茨或其他党内领导人说的话都无法安慰马吉或朱莉，或任何我交谈过的紧张的民主党人。同样住在布劳沃德的杰夫·格林（Jeff Green）记得，两年前，他们所有人是多么自信地投入州长选举。那是本应显示民主党可以在佛罗里达赢得州级职位的竞选。

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:44.330
<v Jeff Green>So two years ago today, Andrew Gillum was up 9 points against Ron DeSantis in the governor's race. And all the Democrats were, ra, ra, ra, Gillum's going to win. And of course, he didn't.
<v Jeff Green>两年前的今天，安德鲁·吉勒姆（Andrew Gillum）在州长竞选中对罗恩·德桑蒂斯（Ron DeSantis）领先9个百分点。所有的民主党人都在喊，加油，吉勒姆要赢了。当然，他没有赢。

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:52.400
<v Lina Misitzis>Looming even bigger, of course, is Hillary Clinton. Every Democrat I talked to invoked Hillary Clinton.
<v Lina Misitzis>当然，更引人注目的是希拉里·克林顿。我交谈过的每一个民主党人都援引了希拉里·克林顿。

00:16:52.400 --> 00:17:00.770
<v Kate>We all remember. We all remember what happened in 2016. We remember what happened in 2018. We remember all of it. And so nobody trusts it anymore.
<v Kate>我们都记得。我们都记得2016年发生了什么。我们记得2018年发生了什么。我们记得所有这些。所以没人再信任它了。

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:25.960
<v Lina Misitzis>Fact is, most big elections in Florida in the last 10 years have been decided by about 1.1% of the vote. Biden's ahead right now in the polls, but not by much. And either candidate could win. So they're not wrong to be worried.
<v Lina Misitzis>事实是，过去10年佛罗里达的大型选举大多由约1.1%的选票决定。拜登目前在民调中领先，但优势不大。任何候选人都有可能获胜。所以他们担心并非没有道理。

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:34.470
<v Ira Glass>Lina Misitzis is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>莉娜·米西齐斯是我们节目的制片人之一。

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:49.390
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, fears of toys that come on without anybody touching them and other worries that have nothing at all to do with electoral politics, I promise. That is in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，关于没人碰就会自己启动的玩具的恐惧，以及其他与选举政治完全无关的担忧，我保证。一分钟后，我们的节目继续。

00:17:49.390 --> 00:18:02.960
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, The Moment After This Moment. We have stories today of people who are worrying about a future that is on its way. We are now at Act 2 of our program. Act 2, Words Versus Fear.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目是《那一刻之后》。我们今天讲述关于担心即将到来的未来的人们的故事。我们现在进入节目的第二幕。第二幕，文字与恐惧。

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:19.080
<v Ira Glass>So when you're scared about things that might happen, the most effective thing to do, of course, is to jump into action and see that things go your way, right? To try to make things happen so that the worst does not occur. But the truth is, that doesn't always do the job, right? It doesn't always get rid of your fears. So then what do you do?
<v Ira Glass>所以，当你害怕可能会发生的事情时，最有效的做法当然是采取行动，确保事情朝着你想要的方向发展，对吧？努力让事情发生，以免最坏的情况出现。但事实是，这并不总能奏效，对吧？它并不总能消除你的恐惧。那么你该怎么办？

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:43.340
<v Ira Glass>Well, years ago, we did a story about somebody who came up with this strategy. There were things that this guy was afraid of, things that put him on edge. And one day, he just decided to write them down, just to get them out of his system. And so he started writing, numbering every fear, one after another. Quickly, it got to 10 fears, and then 20, and then 30 and 40 and 50, until he had 138 of them on paper.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，多年前，我们做过一个关于某个人想出这个策略的故事。这个家伙害怕一些事情，一些让他紧张的事情。有一天，他决定把它们写下来，只是为了把它们从脑海中排解出去。于是他开始书写，一个接一个地给每个恐惧编号。很快，到了10个恐惧，然后20个、30个、40个、50个，直到他在纸上列了138个。

00:18:43.340 --> 00:19:03.050
<v Ira Glass>The man's name is Michael Bernard Loggins. He was in his 50s at the time he did this. He's developmentally disabled. He was living in San Francisco-- still does. And the arts program for adults where he did this, a place called Creativity Explored, published this writing as a little, handwritten, xeroxed zine called Fears of Your Life.
<v Ira Glass>这个人的名字叫迈克尔·伯纳德·洛金斯（Michael Bernard Loggins）。他写下这些时已经50多岁了。他有发育障碍。他住在旧金山——现在依然住在那里。他从事创作的成人艺术项目，一个叫“创意探索”（Creativity Explored）的地方，将这些文字作为一份小型、手写、复印的小册子出版，题为《你生活中的恐惧》（Fears of Your Life）。

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:15.770
<v Ira Glass>And then a few years later, Michael found out there were more fears to tame by turning them into words, and he put out a part 2, a sequel, titled Fears of Your Life, a Whole Brand New One. This one listed 45 fears.
<v Ira Glass>几年后，迈克尔发现通过把恐惧变成文字可以驯服更多的恐惧，他推出了第二部分，即续集，题为《你生活中的恐惧，全新的一个》。这本列出了45个恐惧。

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:33.650
<v Ira Glass>Michael gave us permission to excerpt the two books. And they're just this remarkable, wide-ranging, encyclopedic, Talmudic, memorable tour of human fears. And none of them have to do with politics. And they seem worth going back to today. Actor Tom Wright is our reader.
<v Ira Glass>迈克尔允许我们摘录这两本书。它们是一场关于人类恐惧的非凡、广泛、百科全书式、塔木德式、令人难忘的巡礼。它们没有一个与政治有关。而且它们在今天似乎值得回顾。演员汤姆·赖特（Tom Wright）是我们的读者。

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:49.210
<v Tom Wright>Fear of hospitals and needles. Fear of school and dentists. Fear of noises and bumps in the middle of the night. Fear of doors when they slams. Fear of toys that come on by itself without anyone touching it.
<v Tom Wright>对医院和针头的恐惧。对学校和牙医的恐惧。对半夜的声音和撞击声的恐惧。对门砰砰关上的恐惧。对玩具没人碰自己启动的恐惧。

00:19:49.210 --> 00:20:16.810
<v Tom Wright>Fear of being caught with another woman after cheating on your wife. Fear of being in wrong places at the wrong time. Fear of dropping your soda as it hit the ground and fizz on you. Fear of tall giraffe. Fear of some birds. Fear of being different.
<v Tom Wright>对出轨被抓的恐惧。对在错误的时间出现在错误地点的恐惧。对苏打水掉到地上溅到你的恐惧。对高长颈鹿的恐惧。对某些鸟类的恐惧。对与众不同的恐惧。

