00:44:13.080 --> 00:44:37.530
<v Ira Glass>And I asked her, wait, so you wouldn't explain, I'm on a break from a relationship? She said no, because it sounds crazy. I told her that Kurt would reveal all of that, which she knew. And she said, yeah, Kurt's way too open. But, quote, "That openness probably just made him more appealing to the ladies."
<v Ira Glass>我问她，等等，所以你不会解释，我正在关系中休息？她说不，因为它听起来很疯狂。我告诉她库尔特会揭露所有那些，她知道的。她说，是的，库尔特太开放了。但，引用，“那种开放大概只会让他对女士们更有吸引力。”

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:48.780
<v Ira Glass>By the end of three months, Kurt started to fall for this woman that he met in Australia-- and this time, really, actually fell for her in a real way. And this made him, of course, feel differently about his relationship with his girlfriend.
<v Ira Glass>三个月结束时，库尔特开始爱上他在澳大利亚遇到的这个女人——这次是真的，实际上以真实的方式爱上了她。而这让他，当然，对他的女朋友的关系有了不同的感觉。

00:44:48.780 --> 00:44:59.090
<v Ira Glass>So he flew back to the States, stayed at their old apartment, and because this is the most comfortable relationship that has ever existed between two people, of course, they dealt with it all right away, very straightforward.
<v Ira Glass>所以他飞回美国，留在了他们旧的公寓里，而且因为这是两个人之间存在过的最舒服的关系，当然，他们处理得很好，非常直率。

00:44:59.090 --> 00:45:11.530
<v Kurt Braunohler>And we woke up in the morning, and we went and got brunch. And we brought it to the park, the way we'd always used to do on Saturdays. And we just sat in the park and talked. And we broke up.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我们早上醒来，我们去吃了早午餐。我们把它带到公园，就像我们以前在周六做的那样。我们只是坐在公园里谈论。我们分手了。

00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:33.160
<v Kurt Braunohler>And that's the thing. I can't point to something that was bad about our relationship, except for knowing that I feel like it had run its course. I mean, we organically got to the point where we both thought this was a good idea.
<v Kurt Braunohler>这就是事情。我不能指出我们的关系有什么坏处，除了知道我觉得它已经走完了过程。我是说，我们有机地到达了我们俩都认为这是一个好主意的点。

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:35.170
<v Ira Glass>This-- the Rumspringa?
<v Ira Glass>这个——Rumspringa？

00:45:35.170 --> 00:45:55.060
<v Kurt Braunohler>It's almost like the relationship had an expiration date, but we didn't know about it. And then all of a sudden, we came up with this really complicated, crazy plan to put the test down at the right place where it would break. And it broke.
<v Kurt Braunohler>它几乎像关系有一个到期日，但我们不知道它。然后突然间，我们想出了这个非常复杂、疯狂的计划在测试放下正确的地方它会断裂。它断裂了。

00:45:55.060 --> 00:46:05.920
<v Ira Glass>His old girlfriend said something similar. She said she'd probably wanted out of the relationship before the Rumspringa, but she was too scared to admit it to herself. The Rumspringa gave them a way to break up.
<v Ira Glass>他的旧女朋友说了类似的事情。她说她大概在Rumspringa之前就想离开这段关系，但她太害怕承认它。Rumspringa给了他们一种分手的方式。

00:46:05.920 --> 00:46:18.550
<v Ira Glass>Kurt says he would not want to do a Rumspringa again, but he came out of this experience believing that it is important to force things to a decision with someone, and that it's healthy for any relationship to be evaluated now and then.
<v Ira Glass>库尔特说他不想再做一次Rumspringa，但他从这种经历中出来相信强迫事情与某人做出决定是很重要的，并且任何关系现在和那时进行评估都是健康的。

00:46:18.550 --> 00:46:40.740
<v Kurt Braunohler>I do have a theory now. I do have a theory about if I do get married in the future, what I think I would want to do is have an agreement that at the end of seven years, we have to get remarried in order for the marriage to continue. But at the end of seven years, it ends. And we can agree to get remarried or not get remarried.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我现在有一个理论。我有一个理论关于如果我未来结婚，我想做的是达成一个协议，在七年结束时，我们必须重新结婚为了婚姻继续。但在七年结束时，它结束。我们可以同意重新结婚或不重新结婚。

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:42.070
<v Ira Glass>Why?
<v Ira Glass>为什么？

00:46:42.070 --> 00:46:49.270
<v Kurt Braunohler>Because then I think you get to choose. And I think it makes the relationship-- would make the relationship stronger.
<v Kurt Braunohler>因为那样我认为你得到选择。而且我认为它让关系——会让关系更强大。

00:46:49.270 --> 00:47:05.380
<v Ira Glass>I don't know what I think of that, because I think, actually, one of the things that's a comfort in marriage is that there isn't a door at seven years. And so if something is messed up in the short term, there's the comfort of knowing like, well, we made this commitment. And so we're just going to work this out.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道我怎么看那个，因为我认为，实际上，婚姻中一种安慰是七年时没有门。所以如果短期内某事乱了，知道，嗯，我们做了这个承诺。所以我们只是要解决这个。

00:47:05.380 --> 00:47:19.720
<v Ira Glass>And even if tonight we're not getting along, or there's something between us doesn't feel right, you have the comfort of knowing, we've got time. We're going to figure this out. And that makes it so much easier. Because you do that through times when you hate each other's guts. You know what I mean?
<v Ira Glass>即使今晚我们相处得不好，或者我们之间某事感觉不对，你有安慰知道，我们有时间。我们要解决这个。这让它容易得多。因为你通过那些你恨不得对方去死的时候那样做。你知道我的意思吗？

00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:21.100
<v Kurt Braunohler>Of course you do, yeah.
<v Kurt Braunohler>当然你做了，是的。

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:29.740
<v Ira Glass>And the no-escape clause, weirdly, is a bigger comfort to being married than I ever would have thought before I got married.
<v Ira Glass>而无逃脱条款，奇怪地，是比我结婚前想象到的对婚姻更大的安慰。

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:30.430
<v Kurt Braunohler>Really?
<v Kurt Braunohler>真的吗？

00:47:30.430 --> 00:47:30.820
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:49.770
<v Kurt Braunohler>I had never thought of it that way. I like thinking about it that way. You just see so many examples of where people don't think that way.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我从来没那样想过。我喜欢那样想。你只是看到这么多人们不那样想的例子。

00:47:49.770 --> 00:48:22.240
<v Ira Glass>But Kurt and his girlfriend feel like the Rumspringa got them where they needed to go as a couple, even if that place was different than they intended when they began the whole thing. Sometimes what you need to do next as a couple, you can't even tell what it should be. And the best you can do is hold your breath, make a guess, and jump.
<v Ira Glass>但库尔特和他的女朋友觉得Rumspringa让他们到达了他们需要去的地方作为夫妻，即使那个地方不同于他们开始整个事情时打算的地方。有时候你需要做接下来作为夫妻的事情，你甚至不能分辨它应该是什么。你能做的最好的是屏住呼吸，做出猜测，然后跳。

00:48:22.240 --> 00:48:55.140
<v Ira Glass>Well, these days, Kurt Braunohler is the host of a podcast called Bananas, and he virtually hosts the long-running variety show with Kristen Schaal, Hot Tub with Kurt and Kristen, Mondays on Twitch. So that story is a few years old. And I could probably say a lot about the things that Kurt and I talk about at the end, but I will leave it at this-- these days, Kurt is married, and I'm not.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，这些日子，库尔特·布劳诺勒是播客《香蕉》（Bananas）的主持人，他与克里斯汀·沙尔（Kristen Schaal）虚拟主持长期的综艺节目，《与库尔特和克里斯汀的热水澡》，每周一在Twitch上。所以那个故事是几年前的。我大概可以说很多关于库尔特和我最后谈论的事情，但我会留下它——这些日子，库尔特结婚了，而我没。

00:48:55.140 --> 00:49:14.980
<v Ira Glass>Act 4, POTUS Operandi. OK, I'll say it. I believe that children are our future. And I believe that worries for our children and their future are probably the most common worries out there. Humanity's number one worry category, top of the charts, our survey says can't be beat, generation after generation.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，总统作风。好吧，我会说。我相信孩子们是我们的未来。我相信对我们孩子和他们未来的担忧大概是最普遍的担忧。人类第一大担忧类别，图表顶部，我们的调查说不可被打败，一代又一代。

00:49:14.980 --> 00:49:33.250
<v Ira Glass>But there's a whole other class of thoughts about our kids' futures that are less-- I don't know. They're not worry, exactly. They're more just like curiosity. 10 years ago, one of our producers back then, Jonathan Menjivar, talked about both those feelings, the curiosity and the worry, on our show.
<v Ira Glass>但还有一整类关于我们孩子未来的想法没那么——我不知道。它们不是担心，完全。它们更多只是好奇。10年前，我们当时的制片人之一，乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar），在我们的节目上谈论了这两种感觉，好奇心和担心。

00:49:33.250 --> 00:49:45.370
<v Jonathan Menjivar>POTUS will have dark wavy hair. Her eyes will be brown, unless they're green, or maybe hazel. She will have full lips. She will not be tall, which is a nice way of saying that she might be short.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>总统会有深色的波浪发。她的眼睛是棕色的，除非它们是绿色的，或者大概是榛色的。她会有丰满的嘴唇。她不会很高，这是一个很好的方式说她可能会很矮。

00:49:45.370 --> 00:50:09.010
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Because of this fact, POTUS will not like going to concerts because, like my wife Hillary, she'll only be able to see the backs of other people's heads and that spot on a person's back between their shoulder blades. Someday when she touches that spot on a person she loves, it will feel oddly familiar and comforting. Because all her life, while standing in line at the swings or waiting for a cashier to ring up her milk, she will have looked at that spot on other people.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>由于这个事实，总统不会喜欢去音乐会，因为，像我的妻子希拉里，她只能看到别人的头的后面和那个人后背在肩胛骨之间的地方。有一天当她摸到她爱的人身上的那个点时，它会感觉奇怪地熟悉和安慰。因为她一生中，当站在秋千队里时或等待收银员算她的奶费时，她会一直看着别人身上的那个点。

00:50:09.010 --> 00:50:21.120
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Because she will be our daughter, POTUS will like chocolate ice cream and enjoy puns. She will need glasses by third grade.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>因为她会是我们的女儿，总统会喜欢巧克力冰淇淋和享受双关语。她会在三年级前需要眼镜。

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:34.180
<v Jonathan Menjivar>POTUS, I should explain, is what Hillary and I have taken to calling the little girl Hillary will give birth to in 2010. Calling our kid "it" only felt right for about a week or two. So we brainstormed baby code names one night in bed.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>总统，我应该解释，是我们希拉里和我在2010年希拉里将生下的小女孩开始称呼。称呼我们的孩子“它”只有大约一周或两周感觉对。所以我们一天晚上在床上头脑风暴婴儿代号。

00:50:34.180 --> 00:50:53.110
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Hillary thought the name should be something like that opening scene in the West Wing pilot, the one where Rob Lowe walks out of the shower, and a woman in underwear and a men's dress shirt reads from his pager, "POTUS in a bicycle accident." Hillary said our code name should pop like that. But we couldn't think of anything as cool as POTUS, so we just stole it.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>希拉里认为名字应该是类似那场《白宫风云》（West Wing）首映式的那个场景，罗伯·洛（Rob Lowe）走出淋浴，和一个穿着内衣和男人礼服衬衫的女人从他的传呼机读，“总统发生自行车事故”。希拉里说我们的代号应该像那样弹出。但我们不能想到任何像总统一样酷的东西，所以我们只是偷了它。

00:50:53.110 --> 00:51:19.780
<v Jonathan Menjivar>I worried that while POTUS was still nothing more than cells splitting into other cells that we might be setting unachievable expectations by naming her the acronym the Secret Service used to refer to the President of the United States. When people find out you're having a baby, they predict all kinds of things about the child you'll have. But I wanted to let POTUS tell us who she was, so I tried to cling mostly to the facts.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我担心当总统还不是什么超过分裂成其他细胞的细胞时，我们可能会通过把她命名为特勤局用来指代美国总统的缩写来设定无法实现的高度期望。当人们发现你要有个孩子时，他们预测你将会有什么样的孩子。但我想要让总统告诉我们她是谁，所以我尝试 cling 大多 cling 到事实。

00:51:19.780 --> 00:51:40.780
<v Jonathan Menjivar>20 weeks into Hillary's pregnancy, we went in for an ultrasound. Our ultrasound technician looked like Kate from Jon & Kate Plus 8 in scrubs. She had that crazy hairdo and everything. We asked if she could tell the gender of the baby. And she said, it's a girl. She said it with the same level of enthusiasm I used to tell Hillary that we got a gas bill in the mail.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>希拉里的怀孕20周，我们进去进行超声检查。我们的超声技术员看起来像穿着刷手服的《强与凯特+8》（Jon & Kate Plus 8）里的凯特（Kate）。她有那个疯狂的发型和所有东西。我们问她是否能分辨婴儿的性别。她说，是女孩。她用同样程度的热情说出它，我过去告诉希拉里我们收到邮件里的煤气费单时的那种热情。

00:51:40.780 --> 00:51:54.900
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Knowing that POTUS was a girl meant that now there was a shape to the mystery. The ultrasound gave us the lines of a coloring book, and we couldn't help but fill them in. Hillary remembered all the stuff about dominant and recessive genes that I slept through in my high school biology class.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>知道总统是女孩意味着现在神秘有了一个形状。超声波给了我们涂色书的线条，我们不禁填充它们。希拉里记得所有关于我在高中生物课上睡过头的显性基因和隐性基因的东西。

00:51:54.900 --> 00:52:13.000
<v Jonathan Menjivar>And we thought about our friend Michelle, a biologist who spends her days working with fruit fly genes. I don't really understand what it is she does exactly, except that it has something to do with diabetes. And this summer, when I was having a fruit fly infestation, she didn't have any suggestions for how I could capture or kill them.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我们想到了我们的朋友米歇尔，一位生物学家，她花天工作水果蝇基因。我并不真正理解她确切做什么，除了它与糖尿病有关。而且这个夏天，当我遭遇果蝇侵扰时，她没有任何建议关于我如何能捕捉或杀死它们。

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:38.350
<v Jonathan Menjivar>I thought maybe Michelle could help us figure out what POTUS might look like. She said she'd do her best, but that this stuff was more complicated in humans than what I was imagining. She also told me that there was a great paper published in Science recently about the genetics of dog coats, and that if we were breeding a dog instead of a little girl, she could be really helpful.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我想大概米歇尔能帮助我们弄清楚总统看起来可能像什么。她说她会尽力，但这东西在人类身上比我想象的更复杂。她还告诉我，《科学》（Science）最近发表了一篇关于狗皮毛遗传学的精彩论文，如果我们是在培育一只狗而不是一个小女孩，她会非常有帮助。

00:52:38.350 --> 00:52:56.520
<v Jonathan Menjivar>So Michelle came over to our house, and in exchange for lasagna and a beer, she sat with me and drew Punnett squares, those little squares divided into four smaller squares that are supposed to help you figure out what traits win over others. Michelle was willing to commit to a few things, like the fact that POTUS will likely have dark hair.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>所以米歇尔来到我们家，为了换取烤宽面条和啤酒，她和我坐在一起画旁尼特方格（Punnett squares），那些分成四个小方格的小方格旨在帮助你弄清楚什么特征赢过其他。米歇尔愿意承诺几件事，比如总统可能拥有深色头发的事实。

00:52:56.520 --> 00:53:12.300
<v Jonathan Menjivar>But really, she said, even if she was able to study me and Hillary in a lab, she couldn't tell us with any certainty what our baby will look like. Because unlike dogs, humans haven't been bred for certain traits, so there's really no way of knowing what green eyes may be hiding in my genes.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>但真的，她说，即使她能在实验室研究我和希拉里，她不能确定地告诉我们我们的婴儿看起来像什么。因为不像狗，人类没有被培育特定特征，所以真的没有办法知道绿色眼睛可能隐藏在我的基因中。

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:29.590
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Mostly, she said, the whole dominant/recessive thing could help us figure out dopey stuff. Like, for instance, now we know that POTUS will have unattached earlobes and be able to impress her friends with her ability to roll her tongue like a taco.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>大多，她说，整个显性/隐性东西能帮助我们弄清楚笨东西。比如，例如，现在我们知道总统会有未连接的耳垂，并将能够用她滚舌头成塔可饼的能力给她的朋友留下深刻印象。

00:53:29.590 --> 00:53:50.110
<v Jonathan Menjivar>A few months ago, not long after we found out that POTUS was a girl, I was riding my bike home from work. The traffic was heavy, so I was going pretty slow. I looked to my right and noticed a girl on the sidewalk looking at me, staring, really. She looked like she was in her early 20s, and she had her auburn hair tied in a thick braid that swung over her right shoulder.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>几个月前，在我们发现总统是女孩后不久，我骑车从工作回家。交通拥堵，所以我走得很慢。我向右看，注意到人行道上的一个女孩看着我，盯着，真的。她看起来像在20多岁，她有她的赤褐色头发绑在一个厚辫子里，摇摆在她的右肩上。

00:53:50.110 --> 00:54:13.500
<v Jonathan Menjivar>I can't remember if she was wearing glasses, but I know she was wearing a dress and holding a petition clipboard in her right hand. If skin was made of Play-Doh, hers is what you would get if you forever mixed the Hillary color with the Jonathan one. I couldn't tell you if it was that or the way she looked at me intently that's to blame, but I stared back. And for a split second, I thought she might be POTUS all grown up.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我不记得她是否戴着眼镜，但我知道她穿着裙子，右手拿着一个请愿剪贴板。如果皮肤是培乐多（Play-Doh）做的，她的就是你永远混合希拉里颜色和乔纳森颜色时会得到的。我不能告诉你如果是那个还是她专注看着我的方式是罪魁祸首，但我盯着回去。有一瞬间，我以为她可能是长大后的总统。

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:45.150
<v Jonathan Menjivar>I wanted to ask her so much, how was school? You live around here? What do you like more, seeing fireflies for the first time in the summer or the first fat snowflakes of winter? Really, though, every question was some variation on the one big question I'll try my whole life to make POTUS answer yes to-- are you happy? It's also, I realize, a question I can't predict the answer to.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>我想问她太多，学校怎么样？你住在附近吗？你喜欢什么多，夏天第一次看到萤火虫还是冬天的第一场大雪花？真的，虽然，每个问题都是我在我一生中会尝试让总统回答“是”的那个大问题的一些变化——你快乐吗？它也是，我意识到，一个我不能预测答案的问题。

00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:56.820
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Menjivar. These days, he's making shows at Pineapple Street Studios. His daughter Sasha is now 10 years old in 5th grade, really into playing the drums, and running for school treasurer.
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·门吉瓦尔。这些日子，他在菠萝街工作室（Pineapple Street Studios）制作节目。他的女儿萨沙（Sasha）现在10岁，在5年级，真正喜欢打鼓，并竞选学校财务主管。

00:54:56.820 --> 00:55:22.840
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "EVERYTHING RIGHT IS WRONG AGAIN," BY THEY MIGHT BE GIANTS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “一切对的都是错的”，由他们可能是巨人（They Might Be Giants）]

00:55:22.840 --> 00:55:42.020
